            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Battle of Gaugamela</strong><br>\nThe Battle of Gaugamela in 331 BC was a decisive victory for Alexander. He faced King Darius III of Persia in what is now northern Iraq. Despite being outnumbered, Alexander's strategic brilliance led to the destruction of the Persian forces. Darius fled, and the road to Babylon was open, marking the end of Persian dominance in the region.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Alexander in Babylon and Persia</strong><br>\nAfter Gaugamela, Alexander took over Babylon, the wealthy Persian capital. He then pushed into Persia, taking over the royal cities of Susa and Persepolis. Persepolis was burned, which some say was in revenge for the Persian burning of Athens. Alexander's control over Persia marked a significant shift in power in the ancient world.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Alexander's Indian Campaign</strong><br>\nAlexander's ambition pushed him further east to India in 326 BC, where he won a difficult victory against King Porus at the Battle of the Hydaspes River. However, his exhausted troops refused to march further into India, marking the eastern limit of Alexander's conquests. This resistance led to his decision to return to Babylon.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
