          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>King and Conqueror</strong><br>\nAs a ruler, Alexander embraced the customs of the conquered lands, wearing Persian clothes and adopting Persian court rituals. He married a Persian woman, Roxana, to cement his relations with his new subjects. His practice of founding cities, like Alexandria, and blending cultures helped to create the Hellenistic world.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Alexander's Death and Legacy</strong><br>\nAlexander died in 323 BC in Babylon at the age of 32. The cause of his death remains a mystery but has been attributed to fever, poisoning, or other ailments. His untimely death left a power vacuum, and his empire was divided among his generals, the Diadochi, leading to a period of wars and turmoil.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Hellenistic Age</strong><br>\nAlexander's conquests spread Greek culture throughout the known world, leading to the Hellenistic Age, which saw the mixing of Greek, Persian, Egyptian, and Indian cultures. This period was marked by advances in science, mathematics, philosophy, and art. Alexander's influence persisted long after his death, shaping the course of history for centuries to come.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
